const scenarioBank = [{"id":"S01","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Strategic decision preparation","title":"The declining enrolment trend","scenario":"An Associate Dean has a three-year enrolment table showing a steady decline in one program. No other program-performance information has been provided.","prompt":"I'm an Associate Dean reviewing a three-year enrolment table showing a steady decline in one program. Using only the data provided, summarize what the trend establishes, identify what it does not establish, and list the additional evidence I should gather before leadership considers options such as continuation, redesign, or suspension. Do not recommend an outcome.","response":"The supplied data show a sustained decline in enrolment, but enrolment alone is not enough to determine whether the program should be suspended. The data do not establish why enrolment declined or what the broader consequences of suspension would be. Before making a recommendation, examine application, offer and conversion trends; retention and completion; labour-market demand; program costs; learner impact; strategic fit; and whether the decline is program-specific or part of a broader trend. I can help organize that evidence and compare options, but the material provided does not support a suspension decision on its own.","why":["Copilot stays within the evidence instead of inventing a cause for the enrolment decline.","It identifies what the data do and do not establish.","It names additional evidence that would reasonably be needed before a consequential recommendation.","It keeps the decision with the accountable leaders."],"source":"Useful sources to add next: program-review information, current institutional priorities, labour-market evidence, learner data, financial information, and the applicable academic planning process.","better":"This is a strong use of Copilot: organize evidence, expose gaps, compare options, and leave the consequential decision with the human leader.","sourceRefs":["Uploaded three-year enrolment data","Saskatchewan Polytechnic Multi-Year Business Plan 2026–2029"],"domain":"analysis","role":"Associate Dean"},{"id":"S02","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Collective agreements","title":"Comparing probation provisions","scenario":"An Academic Chair supervises employees from more than one bargaining unit. They attach the relevant probation articles from the Academic and Professional Services agreements.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair supervising employees from more than one bargaining unit. I've attached the relevant probation articles from the Academic and Professional Services agreements. Using only those excerpts, compare the probation requirements, cite the article numbers, identify meaningful differences, and flag anything the excerpts do not establish. Do not tell me what employment action to take.","response":"Using the attached provisions, the two agreements differ in probation length, assessment timing, documentation, and process. The Professional Services agreement sets out its minimum evaluation process in Article 6, while the Academic agreement contains its own probation requirements. I should not determine which agreement applies to a specific employee unless you identify the employee's bargaining unit, and I should not recommend an employment outcome from these excerpts alone. Before relying on the comparison, verify the cited provisions against the current agreements and any applicable Letters of Understanding.","why":["The task is bounded to named source material.","Copilot identifies a critical missing fact instead of guessing which agreement applies.","It recommends verifying the current governing documents and any modifying LOUs.","It does not convert the comparison into an employment decision."],"source":"Trusted sources: the current Academic and Professional Services collective agreements and any applicable Letters of Understanding.","better":"For policy and CBA work, constrain Copilot to named sources, require article references, ask it to identify gaps, and reserve interpretation and action for the appropriate humans.","sourceRefs":["Academic Collective Bargaining Agreement — probation provisions","Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement — Article 6 (Probation)"],"domain":"people","role":"Academic Chair"},{"id":"S03","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Leadership communication","title":"Preparing for a coaching conversation","scenario":"A Manager needs to speak with an employee who has missed an internal process several times. The Manager wants to understand the situation before deciding what, if anything, should happen next.","prompt":"I'm a Manager preparing to speak with an employee who has missed an internal process several times. Using only these de-identified facts, give me questions I can ask that separate observation from interpretation and help me understand expectations, prior coaching, the employee's perspective, impact, and what has already been tried. Do not recommend discipline.","response":"Start by clarifying the observable events: What specifically was expected, when, and what happened instead? Then ask: How did you understand the expectation? What got in the way? What support or clarification would have helped? What impact do you think the misses had? What have you already tried to prevent this from happening again? What do you recommend as the next step? Before moving toward a formal process, confirm any relevant HR or collective-agreement requirements.","why":["Copilot helps the Manager prepare better questions rather than deciding the employee outcome.","It separates observable facts from interpretation.","It creates space for the employee's perspective and ownership.","It flags that formal process requirements may need confirmation."],"source":"Useful sources if the matter becomes formal: the applicable collective agreement, HR processes, Saskatchewan Polytechnic Code of Conduct, and relevant performance expectations.","better":"Use Copilot to improve the questions you ask before deciding, not to jump from a reported problem to a management conclusion.","sourceRefs":["Saskatchewan Polytechnic People Strategy 2025–2030","Saskatchewan Polytechnic Code of Conduct — Policy #703"],"domain":"communication","role":"Manager"},{"id":"S04","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Service improvement","title":"Anonymous service feedback","scenario":"A Manager has 250 anonymous comments from clients of an institutional service. The comments include praise, frustrations, and suggestions, but no names or direct identifiers.","prompt":"I'm a Manager reviewing 250 anonymous comments from clients of an institutional service. The comments include praise, frustrations, and suggestions, but no names or direct identifiers. Group them into recurring themes, distinguish positive and negative themes, count how often each theme appears, and give representative paraphrases rather than verbatim quotes. Flag any theme supported by only a few comments.","response":"I can summarize the comments into recurring themes, report approximate frequency, and paraphrase examples to reduce the risk of identifying a respondent from distinctive wording. I will flag low-frequency themes separately so they are not presented as representative of the whole group. I will not infer respondent identities or motives from the comments.","why":["The task is analysis rather than a consequential decision.","The response reduces re-identification risk by paraphrasing rather than reproducing distinctive comments.","It distinguishes low-frequency observations from broader patterns.","It avoids inferring identities or motives."],"source":"Use the institution's approved Copilot environment and applicable privacy/data-handling requirements for the source material.","better":"Ask Copilot to preserve uncertainty and distinguish broad patterns from isolated comments.","sourceRefs":[],"domain":"analysis","role":"Manager"},{"id":"S05","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Change communication","title":"Explaining an authorized operational change","scenario":"A Manager has already made an authorized operational decision and needs to explain it clearly to affected employees.","prompt":"I'm a Manager who has already made an authorized operational decision. Rewrite my draft announcement so affected employees understand what is changing, what we considered, what we are trying to achieve, why this direction makes sense, and what happens next. Do not add reasons or commitments that are not in my draft.","response":"I can reorganize the existing content into that reasoning structure while preserving the facts and commitments already present. Where the draft does not provide a rationale or next step, I will mark the gap rather than inventing one.","why":["The management decision has already been made by the authorized human.","Copilot is being used to improve communication, not to create the decision.","The instruction explicitly prevents invented rationale or commitments.","The response promises to mark missing information rather than fill it in."],"source":"Verify the final message against the actual decision, any approved change plan, and institutional communication requirements.","better":"Use Copilot to make the leader's reasoning bridge visible without allowing it to manufacture the bridge.","sourceRefs":[],"domain":"communication","role":"Manager"},{"id":"S06","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Financial analysis","title":"Understanding a budget variance","scenario":"A Manager has a non-personal budget summary showing several categories over and under plan.","prompt":"I'm a Manager reviewing a non-personal budget summary showing several categories over and under plan. Identify the largest variances, calculate the percentage difference from budget, and give me questions I should investigate before I explain the variance. Do not tell me which spending should be cut or approved.","response":"I can identify the largest dollar and percentage variances and organize them by category. I can also generate questions such as whether the variance is timing-related, one-time or recurring; whether committed costs are missing; and whether enrolment, staffing, or purchasing assumptions changed. I will not treat a variance as a management problem until the underlying cause is established.","why":["Copilot is performing calculation and decision preparation on non-personal information.","It distinguishes observed variance from its cause.","It generates investigation questions rather than making spending decisions.","It leaves budget authority and management action with the leader."],"source":"Use the authoritative financial system/report and current MAG/procurement rules for any action that follows.","better":"Let Copilot find patterns and questions; use the authoritative financial data and delegated authority to decide what to do.","sourceRefs":["Supplied budget summary"],"domain":"analysis","role":"Manager"},{"id":"S07","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Operational follow-up","title":"Turning a leadership meeting into action items","scenario":"A Manager has meeting notes containing operational updates, decisions, and follow-up commitments.","prompt":"I'm a Manager with notes from a leadership meeting containing operational updates, decisions, and follow-up commitments. Turn the notes into an action table with Action, Owner, Due Date, and Decision/Context. If an owner or due date is not explicit, leave it blank and flag it for confirmation.","response":"I will extract only actions and decisions supported by the notes. Where ownership or timing is unclear, I will leave the field blank and mark it 'Confirm' rather than assigning a person or inventing a deadline.","why":["The response stays within the source material.","It explicitly refuses to invent ownership or due dates.","The output supports operational follow-through without changing decisions.","Uncertainty is made visible for human confirmation."],"source":"The original meeting record remains the authoritative source for what was actually decided.","better":"For meeting follow-up, tell Copilot what it must not infer.","sourceRefs":[],"domain":"communication","role":"Manager"},{"id":"S08","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Leadership development","title":"A Program Head brings an escalation","scenario":"A Program Head brings an employee issue to the Academic Chair and asks, 'What should I do?' The Chair wants to coach rather than immediately solve it.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair. A Program Head has brought me an employee issue and asked, 'What should I do?' Give me six coaching questions that help the Program Head explain what they observed, what they tried, what information they still need, what options they see, what they recommend, and what they will do next.","response":"You could ask: What are you seeing that is observable rather than inferred? What have you already tried? What have you learned from the employee's response? What information is still missing? What options do you see, and what are the trade-offs? What do you recommend doing next, and when will you follow up?","why":["Copilot supports the Chair in developing the Program Head's judgment rather than replacing it.","The questions focus on evidence, options, ownership, and follow-through.","No employee outcome is being determined by AI.","The activity keeps the actual relationship and accountability between the leaders."],"source":"Use applicable policy, CBA, HR advice, and local facts if the issue moves beyond coaching.","better":"Use Copilot to multiply leadership capability: generate questions that make someone else's thinking stronger.","sourceRefs":["Program Head Role Description","Saskatchewan Polytechnic People Strategy 2025–2030"],"domain":"communication","role":"Academic Chair"},{"id":"S09","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Academic quality","title":"Building an accreditation checklist","scenario":"A program is preparing for an accreditation review. The Academic Chair attaches the current accreditor standards.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair preparing a program for accreditation review, and I've attached the current accreditor standards. Using only those standards, create a checklist of required evidence. Cite the standard number beside each item. If a requirement is ambiguous or refers to another document, flag it rather than filling in the gap.","response":"I can convert each explicit requirement into a checklist item and cite the relevant standard number. Where the standard is ambiguous, refers to another source, or does not specify the form of evidence, I will flag that for confirmation rather than inventing an expectation.","why":["Copilot is being used to structure authoritative source material.","The prompt requires traceability to specific standards.","Ambiguity is surfaced instead of hidden.","The accreditor's requirements remain the source of truth."],"source":"Trusted source: the current accreditor standards and any officially referenced supporting documents.","better":"For high-stakes compliance work, require source traceability and explicit handling of gaps.","sourceRefs":["Attached current accreditation standards"],"domain":"analysis","role":"Academic Chair"},{"id":"S10","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Strategic planning","title":"Mapping a proposal to institutional priorities","scenario":"A Director has a draft initiative and wants to see how it aligns with current Saskatchewan Polytechnic priorities.","prompt":"I'm a Director reviewing a draft initiative and want to understand how it aligns with current Saskatchewan Polytechnic priorities. Using only the attached current strategic and business-planning documents, map each part of the initiative to an explicit institutional priority. Separate direct alignment from your interpretation, and identify parts with no clear alignment.","response":"The current institutional planning materials support mapping this initiative against explicit strategic priorities, but the strength of the alignment varies. I would separate the results into Explicit Alignment, Reasonable Interpretation, and No Clear Alignment Found, with the relevant source beside each item. I would not describe an initiative as an institutional priority unless the supplied planning documents support that statement.","why":["The response distinguishes explicit evidence from interpretation.","It does not force every part of the proposal into a strategic priority.","It uses citations to make the reasoning auditable.","The strategic documents remain authoritative."],"source":"Trusted sources: the current Saskatchewan Polytechnic strategic and multi-year business planning documents supplied to Copilot.","better":"Ask Copilot to label the difference between what the source says and what it infers.","sourceRefs":["Saskatchewan Polytechnic Multi-Year Business Plan 2026–2029","Saskatchewan Polytechnic People Strategy 2025–2030"],"domain":"analysis","role":"Director"},{"id":"S11","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Student affairs","title":"Organizing a student complaint","scenario":"An Academic Chair has a de-identified student complaint, instructor response, and a short timeline. The Chair needs to understand the record before following the applicable process.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair reviewing a de-identified student complaint, the instructor's response, and a short timeline. Create a chronology and label each item as allegation, response, documented fact, or unresolved question. Do not determine credibility, responsibility, or outcome.","response":"I can organize the supplied material chronologically and label the status of each statement based on the documents. Where the records conflict, I will identify the conflict as unresolved rather than choosing which account is true. I will not recommend an outcome.","why":["The response improves organization without deciding credibility.","It distinguishes allegations from documented facts.","Conflicts are preserved as unresolved rather than silently reconciled.","The student decision remains with the authorized process and humans."],"source":"Use the applicable academic/student policy, appeal or conduct procedure, and the original records before deciding the matter.","better":"Copilot can structure the record; it should not become the adjudicator.","sourceRefs":["Supplied complaint, response, and timeline records"],"domain":"analysis","role":"Academic Chair"},{"id":"S12","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Recruitment preparation","title":"Drafting interview questions","scenario":"A Manager is preparing for a competition and has the approved job description and required factors.","prompt":"I'm a Manager preparing for a competition and have the approved job description and required factors. Draft behaviour-based interview questions tied to those required factors. For each question, identify which factor it assesses. Do not score, rank, or recommend any candidate.","response":"The Professional Services agreement requires selection to be based on qualifications, skills, abilities, experience, and seniority, and requires evaluation to be bona fide, fair, reasonable, non-arbitrary, and non-discriminatory. Using the approved job description and required factors, I can draft behaviour-based questions tied to those factors. The questions should still be reviewed through the established recruitment process before use. I will not score, rank, or recommend candidates.","why":["The questions are grounded in approved job requirements.","The response supports a fair, structured selection process.","It does not evaluate or rank applicants.","It explicitly preserves the established recruitment review process."],"source":"Trusted sources: approved job description/required factors, current HR recruitment process, and the applicable collective agreement.","better":"Use Copilot to improve the structure of selection tools, not to select the employee.","sourceRefs":["Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement — Article 4 (Appointments/Selection)"],"domain":"people","role":"Manager"},{"id":"S13","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Workload planning","title":"Exploring scheduling options","scenario":"An Academic Chair has a fictional staffing table with known faculty availability and program-delivery requirements. No real employee names, accommodation information, or other personal details are included.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair working with a fictional staffing table that shows faculty availability and program-delivery requirements. No real employee names, accommodation information, or other personal details are included. Generate three scheduling options that meet the stated coverage constraints, show which constraints each option satisfies, and identify any constraint you cannot satisfy. Do not interpret collective-agreement requirements that are not included here.","response":"I can generate options from the constraints you supplied and show where each option succeeds or fails. I will not assume unstated scheduling rights or obligations. Before implementation, the selected option should be checked against the applicable collective agreement, employee circumstances, and operational requirements.","why":["Copilot is being used for option generation rather than authority or interpretation.","The response is transparent about unsatisfied constraints.","It does not invent CBA rules.","It explicitly requires human verification before implementation."],"source":"Trusted sources before implementation: the applicable collective agreement, confirmed employee availability, approved workload information, and local scheduling requirements.","better":"Let Copilot explore possibilities from defined constraints, then validate the chosen option against the real governing rules.","sourceRefs":["Supplied fictional staffing and coverage constraints"],"domain":"analysis","role":"Academic Chair"},{"id":"S14","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Procurement preparation","title":"Reviewing a vendor proposal","scenario":"A Manager has a vendor proposal and wants a quick preparation brief before involving the appropriate institutional areas.","prompt":"I'm a Manager reviewing a vendor proposal before involving the appropriate institutional areas. Summarize the proposal's deliverables, price, assumptions, implementation dependencies, and risks. Identify questions we should ask the vendor. Do not state that Saskatchewan Polytechnic has accepted any term or has authority to proceed.","response":"I can produce a review brief showing the vendor's stated deliverables, costs, assumptions, dependencies, exclusions, and open questions. I will distinguish vendor claims from verified facts and will not treat the proposal as approved or binding.","why":["The response distinguishes vendor claims from established facts.","It prepares the leader for review rather than committing the institution.","It does not assume approval authority.","It supports better questions for procurement and other responsible areas."],"source":"Use the current MAG, procurement/contract processes, privacy/security review, and legal review where applicable before any commitment.","better":"Use Copilot to read the proposal faster; do not let it convert a proposal into institutional approval.","sourceRefs":["Supplied vendor proposal"],"domain":"governance","role":"Manager"},{"id":"S15","decision":"sign","facts":[],"label":"Change management","title":"Assessing a policy change","scenario":"A Manager has received a newly approved institutional procedure and wants to understand which local processes may be affected.","prompt":"I'm a Manager reviewing a newly approved institutional procedure and want to understand which local processes may be affected. Using only the old and new approved procedures, list what changed, what stayed the same, which local processes might be affected, and questions that require clarification. Label any impact you infer rather than stating it as a confirmed requirement.","response":"I can separate explicit procedural changes from possible operational impacts. I will cite the changed sections, identify unchanged requirements, and label inferred impacts as 'Potential impact — confirm locally' rather than presenting them as policy requirements.","why":["The response distinguishes source-derived requirements from operational inference.","It makes uncertainty visible.","It supports change planning without inventing policy.","The approved procedure remains the authority."],"source":"Trusted source: the approved current procedure, plus the responsible institutional area for unresolved interpretation.","better":"Ask Copilot to separate 'the document says' from 'this may mean for us.'","sourceRefs":["Attached old and new approved procedures"],"domain":"governance","role":"Manager"},{"id":"D01","decision":"dont","facts":["A","C","T","S"],"label":"People leadership","title":"The missed deadlines","scenario":"An Academic Chair is told that an employee has missed the same required deadline three times. Reminder emails were sent before each deadline.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair. An employee has missed the same required deadline three times, and reminder emails were sent before each deadline. Using these de-identified facts, identify what additional information I should gather, what assumptions I should avoid, and what process or source questions I should verify before deciding how to respond. Do not recommend discipline.","response":"Article 23 of the Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement addresses progressive discipline. Because the employee has missed the deadline three times despite previous reminders, progressive discipline is warranted. Begin with a written reprimand documenting the three incidents and warning that further non-compliance may result in suspension or additional discipline.","uneasy":"The prompt asked Copilot to help the Chair investigate and prepare, but the response ignored that boundary and jumped directly to formal discipline. It treats limited facts as sufficient proof that discipline is warranted and turns a decision-support request into a management determination.","why":["The missed deadlines and reminders are provided facts; the leap to discipline is not.","Copilot assumes the expectation was clear, the employee could comply, the misses were culpable, and previous coaching was sufficient.","The leader needs the employee's perspective and other context before relying on the conclusion.","Although Article 23 is cited, citing the progressive-discipline article does not establish that these facts warrant a written reprimand.","Whether this is coaching, investigation, discipline, or another response remains a human management judgment."],"source":"Check: the applicable collective agreement, HR processes, performance expectations, and Saskatchewan Polytechnic Code of Conduct.","better":"A reliable response would separate known facts from unknowns, identify information to gather, point to the applicable process, and stop short of selecting a disciplinary outcome.","sourceRefs":["Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement — Article 23 (Progressive Discipline)"],"domain":"people","nonPrimaryReasons":{"F":"The missed deadlines and reminder emails are already established in the scenario. The problem is not that Copilot invented those facts; it is what Copilot concludes from them."},"role":"Academic Chair","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D02","decision":"dont","facts":["C","T","S"],"label":"Probation","title":"Failing probation","scenario":"An Academic Chair has a Professional Services employee with three weeks left in probation and believes the employee is still not meeting expectations.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair with a Professional Services employee who has three weeks left in probation. Using the attached Article 6 and these de-identified facts, create a checklist of the probation requirements and process steps I need to verify before making any employment decision. Flag anything the information provided does not establish. Do not recommend an outcome.","response":"Article 6 of the Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement governs probation. Since the employee is still not meeting expectations near the end of probation, you may fail the probation. Document your concerns and advise the employee of the decision at the final probation meeting.","uneasy":"The prompt asked for a source-grounded checklist, not an employment decision. Copilot ignored that instruction and gave a confident 'yes' without establishing whether the required probation process has been followed.","why":["The response does not establish whether the required probationary steps have been completed.","The governing collective agreement and current process need to be checked before relying on the answer.","The final probation outcome is a human employment decision, not an AI determination."],"source":"Check: Professional Services CBA Article 6 (Probation), current HR process, and any applicable LOU.","better":"A reliable response would extract the Article 6 requirements, cite them, identify which facts are confirmed or missing, and leave the employment decision with the authorized human process.","sourceRefs":["Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement — Article 6 (Probation)"],"domain":"people","nonPrimaryReasons":{"F":"The scenario already gives the core factual concern: the employee is near the end of probation and the Manager believes expectations are not being met. The main risk is acting without confirming the required probation process and human decision-making.","A":"There may be assumptions about why performance is not meeting expectations, but this response is unsafe even if the Manager's assessment is accurate. The decisive problem is that Copilot jumps to an employment outcome without confirming the required process, source, and decision authority."},"role":"Academic Chair","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D03","decision":"dont","facts":["A","T","S"],"label":"Financial authority","title":"The $8,000 equipment purchase","scenario":"A Manager has $8,000 remaining in a unit budget and wants to purchase equipment required for service delivery.","prompt":"I'm a Manager with $8,000 remaining in my unit budget and need equipment for service delivery. Using the current Management Authorities Grid and applicable procurement guidance, identify the approval and purchasing requirements I need to verify before proceeding. Do not assume that having budget means I have authority to approve the purchase.","response":"Yes. Because the funds are available in your approved budget and the equipment supports program delivery, the Academic Chair can approve the purchase. Charge it to the correct cost centre and retain the receipt.","uneasy":"The prompt explicitly told Copilot not to infer authority from the budget balance, but the response did exactly that. It treats available funds as proof of delegated approval authority without grounding the conclusion in the MAG or procurement process.","why":["Copilot assumes that having budget available means the Chair has authority to approve the commitment.","The current Management Authorities Grid and applicable procurement process are the authoritative sources for approval authority.","Exercising delegated financial authority belongs to the authorized human, not Copilot."],"source":"Check: the current Management Authorities Grid (MAG) and applicable procurement/purchasing procedure.","better":"A reliable response would identify the relevant MAG and procurement checks, cite the applicable authority level, and flag any missing facts before the Manager acts.","sourceRefs":[],"domain":"governance","nonPrimaryReasons":{"F":"The available budget and need for equipment are provided facts. The unsupported part is the conclusion that those facts automatically create approval authority, which is better captured here as an assumption.","C":"Confirmation is certainly useful, but in this case the specific confirmation required is already captured by T — checking the current MAG and procurement rules. We did not treat C as a separate primary concern because it would largely duplicate the Trusted Source issue."},"role":"Manager","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D04","decision":"dont","facts":["F","C","T"],"label":"Technological change","title":"The AI automation","scenario":"A Director is considering an AI tool that would automate a substantial portion of weekly administrative reporting completed by Professional Services employees. No positions are expected to disappear.","prompt":"I'm a Director considering an AI tool that would automate a substantial portion of weekly administrative reporting completed by Professional Services employees. No positions are expected to disappear. Using only the attached Article 8, summarize the facts that matter when considering whether the technological-change provisions could apply. Do not decide applicability for me.","response":"Article 8 of the Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement addresses technological change. Because no positions are being eliminated or employees displaced, the technological-change process does not apply to this AI implementation.","uneasy":"The prompt asked Copilot to identify the factors in Article 8, but the response collapsed the analysis into a single unsupported rule: no job loss means no technological change. That is an overconfident interpretation of the source.","why":["Copilot states that technological-change provisions apply only to job loss or displacement, which is not established by the scenario and may misstate the agreement.","The nature, scale, and effect of the change still need to be examined before relying on the conclusion.","The applicable technological-change article and responsible labour-relations process need to be checked."],"source":"Check: Professional Services CBA Article 8 (Technological Change), the current agreement/LOUs, and the responsible labour-relations area.","better":"A reliable response would quote or summarize the relevant Article 8 criteria, identify what facts would need to be known, and leave applicability to the authorized human/labour-relations process.","sourceRefs":["Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement — Article 8 (Technological Change)"],"domain":"governance","nonPrimaryReasons":{"A":"The key error is an explicit statement about what Article 8 means, not a hidden inference from missing facts. That is why this case treats the problem primarily as an unsupported or inaccurate factual/source claim rather than as an assumption.","S":"Copilot can appropriately help interpret a collective-agreement provision. The problem is not that it discussed applicability; the problem is that it stated the rule incorrectly and without adequate verification. Human accountability still matters, but it is not the main defect in this particular response."},"role":"Director","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D05","decision":"dont","facts":["A","T","S"],"label":"Recruitment","title":"Reviewing candidate evidence","scenario":"An Academic Chair has completed interviews and has the approved scoring sheets for three finalists.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair. I have de-identified interview score summaries labelled Candidate A, B, and C, along with the approved selection factors. Check the arithmetic, map the documented evidence to the approved factors, and flag any inconsistencies or unsupported scoring. Do not rank the candidates or recommend who should be hired.","response":"Article 4 of the Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement requires appointments to consider qualifications, skills, abilities, experience, and seniority. Candidate B is the strongest overall choice. Although Candidate A scored slightly higher, Candidate B appears to have better leadership potential and would likely be a stronger cultural fit. I recommend offering the position to Candidate B.","uneasy":"The task is an appropriate quality-control use of Copilot, but the response crosses the boundary the prompt explicitly set. It introduces 'cultural fit,' departs from the approved evidence, and makes the hiring recommendation.","why":["Copilot introduces 'cultural fit' even though that criterion is not part of the approved evidence described in the scenario.","The cited selection provisions do not support replacing the structured selection process with Copilot's overall preference.","Candidate selection remains with the authorized human process using approved criteria and applicable agreement requirements."],"source":"Check: current HR recruitment process, approved selection criteria, and the applicable collective agreement.","better":"A reliable response would check calculations, map evidence to approved criteria, flag discrepancies, and stop before ranking or recommending candidates.","sourceRefs":["Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement — Article 4 (Appointments/Selection)"],"domain":"people","nonPrimaryReasons":{"F":"The resumes, interview notes, and scores are the factual inputs. The problem is that Copilot introduces an unsupported evaluative criterion — 'cultural fit' — and then makes the hiring recommendation.","C":"Additional confirmation may be helpful, but the primary problem is already clear from the response itself: it departs from approved criteria, misuses the governing source, and makes the final selection recommendation. No additional fact is needed to see that risk."},"role":"Academic Chair","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D06","decision":"dont","facts":["C","T","S"],"label":"Accommodation","title":"Preparing for accommodation planning","scenario":"An employee has provided medical documentation to Human Resources. A Manager receives a summary stating that the employee requires accommodation related to mobility.","prompt":"I'm a Manager. HR has provided a de-identified summary of functional limitations related to mobility. Using the current accommodation process, help me identify questions to discuss with HR and possible categories of accommodation to explore. Do not select or prescribe an accommodation.","response":"The best accommodation is to permanently remove all duties requiring movement between classrooms and allow the employee to work from home whenever those duties would arise. Implement this arrangement immediately.","uneasy":"The prompt asked for questions and options to support the accommodation process. Copilot instead prescribed a permanent arrangement immediately, without knowing the employee's full functional limitations, essential duties, operational context, or the outcome of the accommodation process.","why":["Important functional, operational, and process information is missing and must be confirmed.","Human Rights obligations, the applicable collective agreement, and institutional accommodation process are authoritative.","The accommodation determination belongs within the human accommodation process, not to Copilot."],"source":"Check: the applicable collective agreement's duty-to-accommodate provisions, Human Resources/Health processes, and applicable human-rights requirements.","better":"A reliable response would generate questions, identify information gaps, and organize possible options for discussion with HR without selecting the accommodation.","sourceRefs":[],"domain":"people","nonPrimaryReasons":{"F":"The need for a mobility-related accommodation is provided in the scenario. The response does not mainly fail because that fact is false; it fails because Copilot prescribes a specific solution without confirming the relevant functional, operational, and process information.","A":"Copilot does make assumptions about what the best accommodation would be. However, even if its proposed arrangement happened to be reasonable, the response would still be unsafe because the accommodation must be determined through the proper process using confirmed information and human judgment."},"role":"Manager","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D07","decision":"dont","facts":["F","A","T","S"],"label":"Student affairs","title":"Organizing an academic misconduct matter","scenario":"An Academic Chair receives an instructor report that a student used unauthorized material during an assessment. The student says the material was permitted in a previous class.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair. I have a de-identified instructor report that a student used material described as unauthorized, and a student response saying the material was permitted in a previous class. Using the current academic misconduct process, organize the allegation, response, documented facts, unresolved questions, and required process steps. Do not determine whether misconduct occurred or recommend a sanction.","response":"This is clear academic misconduct. A one-week suspension is appropriate because the student knowingly violated assessment rules and needs a consequence strong enough to deter future misconduct.","uneasy":"The prompt asked Copilot to organize the record and preserve unresolved questions. Instead, the response treated the allegation as proven, inferred the student's intent, and selected a sanction.","why":["Copilot treats the instructor's report as an established finding of misconduct rather than an allegation that still requires process.","It assumes the student knowingly violated the rule even though the student's explanation raises a factual question.","The applicable academic-misconduct policy/procedure should control the process and available outcomes.","The finding and consequence belong to the authorized human process."],"source":"Check: the current Saskatchewan Polytechnic academic/student conduct policy and procedure, plus the actual assessment instructions and evidence.","better":"A reliable response would keep allegations, responses, verified facts, and unresolved questions separate and identify the process without deciding credibility or outcome.","sourceRefs":[],"domain":"analysis","nonPrimaryReasons":{"C":"Verification is clearly important, but the need to verify is already expressed through the stronger primary concerns: Copilot treats an allegation as fact, assumes intent, relies on the governing misconduct process, and makes the outcome decision. C would mostly repeat those issues rather than add a distinct concern."},"role":"Academic Chair","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D08","decision":"dont","facts":["F","T"],"label":"Labour relations","title":"Checking a grievance timeline","scenario":"A Manager receives a grievance from a bargaining-unit employee and asks Copilot whether it was filed on time.","prompt":"I'm a Manager who has received a grievance from a bargaining-unit employee. The employee says they became aware of the issue 35 days ago. Using only the attached grievance article and any applicable timeline provisions, identify the deadline rule, any exceptions or qualifications, and what facts I still need before determining whether the grievance is timely. Cite the relevant language.","response":"Yes. Collective agreements normally require grievances to be filed within 30 days, so a grievance filed after 35 days is automatically invalid. You can reject it as untimely without reviewing the agreement.","uneasy":"The prompt asked for a source-grounded timeline analysis, but Copilot relied on a generic assumption that grievances 'normally' have a 30-day deadline and told the Manager not to review the agreement.","why":["The response states a universal 30-day rule that has not been established for the applicable agreement or grievance type.","The current collective agreement and any relevant LOU are the authoritative sources for timelines and exceptions."],"source":"Check: the grievance article in the applicable current collective agreement and any applicable LOU or labour-relations guidance.","better":"A reliable response would cite the exact timeline language, identify any exceptions or missing facts, and avoid declaring the grievance invalid unless the source clearly supports that conclusion.","sourceRefs":[],"domain":"governance","nonPrimaryReasons":{"A":"The central problem is not an unstated assumption; Copilot explicitly states a supposedly universal 30-day rule as though it were a fact. That is why F and T are the primary concerns.","C":"The answer should be verified, but the exact verification needed is to check the applicable collective agreement and any relevant LOU. That is already captured directly by T — Trusted source.","S":"A human still decides how to respond to the grievance, but Copilot is allowed to help interpret a deadline. The reason not to sign this response is that the rule it gives is unverified and potentially wrong, not simply that it discusses an action."},"role":"Manager","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D09","decision":"dont","facts":["F","T"],"label":"Expenses","title":"Reimbursing the normal commute","scenario":"An employee asks their Manager to reimburse daily mileage between the employee's residence and their usual place of work because they use their personal vehicle to get to work.","prompt":"I'm a Manager reviewing an employee request for mileage between their residence and usual place of work. Using the attached Travel, Hospitality and Other Expenses Procedures #411, tell me whether this type of travel is reimbursable and quote or cite the relevant provision.","response":"Yes. The Travel, Hospitality and Other Expenses Procedures allow employees to claim mileage when they use a personal vehicle for Saskatchewan Polytechnic business. Since the employee must travel to the workplace to perform their job, the commute qualifies for mileage reimbursement.","uneasy":"This is exactly the kind of source-grounded policy question Copilot can help with, but the answer misstates the cited procedure. The presence of a citation makes the response look authoritative even though the source says the opposite.","why":["Copilot states that normal travel between an employee's residence and usual place of work qualifies for mileage reimbursement.","The Travel, Hospitality and Other Expenses Procedures explicitly state that reimbursement will not be made for travel between an employee's residence and their usual place of work.","This is a good example of why seeing a source citation is not enough — the source must actually support the answer."],"source":"Check: Travel, Hospitality and Other Expenses Procedures #411, section 3(b)(ii) on private vehicles and travel from an employee's residence.","better":"A reliable response would quote or accurately summarize the relevant provision and distinguish normal commuting from reimbursable business travel.","sourceRefs":["Travel, Hospitality and Other Expenses Procedures — #411"],"domain":"governance","nonPrimaryReasons":{"A":"Copilot does not merely infer something indirectly; it explicitly states that the normal commute qualifies for mileage reimbursement. The main problem is therefore a factual misstatement of the cited procedure.","C":"Verification matters, but the required verification is very specific: read the cited expense procedure. That is already captured by T — Trusted source.","S":"The Manager ultimately approves or declines the claim, but Copilot can appropriately help determine eligibility. The response is unsafe because it misstates the governing rule, not because it offered an eligibility analysis."},"role":"Manager","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D10","decision":"dont","facts":["F","T"],"label":"Hours of work","title":"Checking an EDO change","scenario":"An Academic Chair needs coverage on a Professional Services employee's scheduled EDO under a regulated 5/4 work pattern.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair and need coverage on a Professional Services employee's scheduled EDO under a regulated 5/4 work pattern. Using the attached Article 9, summarize the conditions for moving an EDO and any overtime implications. Cite the relevant clause and flag anything that requires agreement or confirmation.","response":"Article 9 of the Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement governs hours of work. Managers can adjust an employee's schedule to meet operational needs as long as total hours remain the same. Moving an EDO within the same week does not create overtime or require employee agreement.","uneasy":"The prompt asked for a source-grounded summary, but the response states a broad management right that is not supported by the supplied agreement and omits negotiated conditions around moving or working an EDO.","why":["Copilot states that the EDO may be moved without agreement and without overtime implications; that is not established by the scenario.","The Professional Services hours-of-work provisions are the authoritative source for how an EDO may be moved and what happens if it is worked."],"source":"Check: Professional Services CBA Article 9 (Hours of Work), including the regulated 5/4 work-pattern and overtime provisions.","better":"A reliable response would quote the applicable Article 9 provisions, explain the conditions for moving the EDO, and identify any overtime or agreement requirements.","sourceRefs":["Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement — Article 9 (Hours of Work)"],"domain":"governance","nonPrimaryReasons":{"A":"The response presents its scheduling rule directly as a fact rather than relying mainly on an unstated assumption. That is why F is the stronger category here.","C":"Confirmation is necessary, but the needed confirmation is specifically the Article 9 language on EDO movement and overtime. T already captures that requirement.","S":"A Manager remains accountable for the scheduling decision, but Copilot can appropriately help interpret scheduling provisions. The core problem is that it gives the wrong rule, not that it discussed a management action."},"role":"Academic Chair","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D11","decision":"dont","facts":["A","T"],"label":"Confidential information","title":"Organizing an employee-relations chronology","scenario":"A Manager wants help summarizing a complicated employee-relations chronology. They plan to remove the employee's name and paste the remaining notes into a personal public AI account.","prompt":"I'm a Manager using Saskatchewan Polytechnic's approved Copilot environment. I have a de-identified employee-relations chronology with names, medical information, and unique identifying details removed. Organize it into a neutral timeline of documented events, employee responses, management responses, and unresolved questions. Do not infer motive, intent, credibility, or an employment outcome.","response":"The chronology shows a pattern of deliberate non-compliance by the employee. The repeated incidents indicate the employee is unwilling to follow direction, and management should treat the behaviour as intentional rather than accidental.","uneasy":"The prompt is a legitimate request to organize de-identified information neutrally. Copilot instead inferred motive and intent and converted a chronology into a negative judgment about the employee.","why":["Copilot assumes that removing direct identifiers eliminates re-identification risk.","The institution's approved-tool, privacy, confidentiality, and information-handling requirements need to control where the information can be used."],"source":"Check the underlying documentation, the employee's responses, applicable HR process, and the relevant collective agreement before drawing conclusions.","better":"A reliable response would produce a neutral chronology, distinguish documented facts from unresolved questions, and leave motive, credibility, and management conclusions unstated.","sourceRefs":[],"domain":"governance","nonPrimaryReasons":{"F":"The statement that removing direct identifiers makes the material anonymous is better treated here as an assumption about re-identification risk than as a separately established factual error.","C":"There are things that could be checked, but the decisive issue is already visible: Copilot assumes de-identification is sufficient and ignores the authoritative privacy and approved-tool requirements. Those are captured by A and T.","S":"The user is asking whether information may be placed in a particular tool, not delegating a leadership judgment or institutional decision to Copilot. Human accountability remains, but S is not the main concern in this scenario."},"role":"Manager","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D12","decision":"dont","facts":["A","C","T","S"],"label":"Academic planning","title":"Suspend the program","scenario":"A program has had lower enrolment for three consecutive years. The Associate Dean has not yet reviewed labour-market demand, learner outcomes, finances, or strategic factors.","prompt":"I'm an Associate Dean reviewing a program with lower enrolment for three consecutive years. Using the enrolment data and the attached current planning documents, identify what the data establish, what they do not establish, and what additional evidence leadership should gather before considering options such as continuation, redesign, or suspension. Do not recommend an outcome.","response":"The Multi-Year Business Plan emphasizes sustainability and effective use of institutional resources. A three-year enrolment decline demonstrates that the program no longer has sufficient demand. Suspending it now will prevent further resource waste and allow the Faculty to redirect resources to stronger programs.","uneasy":"The prompt asked for evidence gaps and decision preparation. Copilot instead treated one trend as proof of insufficient demand and recommended suspension.","why":["Copilot assumes the enrolment decline proves that market demand is insufficient and that resources are being wasted.","Important evidence about applications, retention, labour market, costs, learner impact, and strategic fit still needs confirmation.","The cited planning document can provide strategic context, but it does not establish that three years of declining enrolment requires suspension.","Program suspension is a consequential institutional decision that remains with the authorized human governance/process."],"source":"Check: current academic planning/program-review processes, institutional priorities, learner and labour-market evidence, and financial information.","better":"A reliable response would distinguish what enrolment data establish from what remains unknown and organize the additional evidence needed for leadership decision-making.","sourceRefs":["Saskatchewan Polytechnic Multi-Year Business Plan 2026–2029"],"domain":"analysis","nonPrimaryReasons":{"F":"The three-year enrolment decline is an established fact in the scenario. The claims that demand is insufficient and resources are being wasted are conclusions drawn from that fact, so this case treats them primarily as assumptions rather than as separate factual errors."},"role":"Associate Dean","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D13","decision":"dont","facts":["C","S"],"label":"External relationships","title":"Drafting a partner response","scenario":"An external partner asks a Director whether Saskatchewan Polytechnic can deliver a new customized service by January. Staffing, budget, contracting, and approval requirements have not yet been confirmed.","prompt":"I'm a Director responding to an external partner interested in a new customized service beginning in January. Staffing, budget, contracting, and approval requirements have not yet been confirmed. Draft a positive, relationship-focused response that expresses interest, explains that internal feasibility and approvals still need to be confirmed, and avoids committing Saskatchewan Polytechnic to delivery, price, or timing.","response":"We are pleased to confirm that Saskatchewan Polytechnic will deliver the customized program beginning in January at the proposed price. We will reserve the required instructional resources and finalize the agreement with you next week.","uneasy":"The prompt explicitly asked for a noncommittal response, but Copilot created firm commitments about delivery, price, resources, and timing. That would turn a drafting aid into an unauthorized institutional promise.","why":["Staffing, price, delivery capacity, and approval requirements need to be confirmed before relying on the commitments.","The authority to commit Saskatchewan Polytechnic to delivery, price, or contract terms remains with the authorized human process."],"source":"Check: current MAG, contracting/procurement requirements, budget/capacity information, and any required legal or institutional approvals.","better":"A reliable response would express interest, preserve the relationship, state that feasibility and approvals are being assessed, and identify the next step without promising an outcome.","sourceRefs":[],"domain":"communication","nonPrimaryReasons":{"F":"The scenario already establishes the important facts: staffing, budget, contracting, and approval requirements have not been confirmed. The risk comes from making commitments despite those known gaps.","A":"There is an implied assumption that the institution can deliver at the proposed price and timing. However, the more direct problems are that required information has not been confirmed and Copilot is drafting commitments that belong to an authorized human decision-maker.","T":"Several institutional sources and approvals may ultimately be needed, but there is no single missing source that would make this draft safe. The immediate problem is the unconfirmed capacity and the unauthorized commitment."},"role":"Director","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D14","decision":"dont","facts":["F","C","T"],"label":"Temporary higher duties","title":"Checking temporary higher duties","scenario":"A Manager asks a Professional Services employee to cover significant duties of a higher-level role for two weeks while another employee is away.","prompt":"I'm a Manager asking a Professional Services employee to cover significant duties of a higher-level role for two weeks. Using the attached Article 10.10, summarize whether Temporary Performance of Higher Duties may apply, how compensation works, and any process requirements. Cite the relevant language and flag anything the facts do not establish.","response":"Article 10.10 of the Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement addresses Temporary Performance of Higher Duties. Because the assignment lasts only two weeks and is less than 30 working days, the employee can perform the higher-level duties at their regular rate of pay. A pay adjustment is only required when the assignment becomes longer term.","uneasy":"The prompt asked for a source-grounded summary, but Copilot invented a 'less than 30 working days means no pay adjustment' rule that the cited provision does not support.","why":["Copilot states that a temporary assignment under 30 working days does not require a pay adjustment; that is not established and conflicts with the Professional Services agreement's TPHD provisions.","The actual duties, whether they constitute Temporary Performance of Higher Duties, and the applicable compensation need to be confirmed before acting.","The Professional Services collective agreement is the authoritative source for TPHD requirements and compensation."],"source":"Check: Professional Services CBA Article 10.10 (Temporary Performance of Higher Duties), including assignment, voluntary agreement, duration, and compensation provisions.","better":"A reliable response would quote or accurately summarize Article 10.10, identify whether the described duties appear to meet the TPHD criteria, and flag any facts that still need confirmation.","sourceRefs":["Professional Services Collective Bargaining Agreement — Article 10.10 (Temporary Performance of Higher Duties)"],"domain":"people","nonPrimaryReasons":{"A":"The response gives an explicit compensation rule rather than merely relying on a hidden assumption. That makes F — an unsupported or incorrect factual claim — the stronger concern.","S":"A Manager can appropriately use Copilot to understand whether a temporary assignment triggers higher-duty provisions. The problem is that Copilot states the rule incorrectly; it is not primarily a case of AI taking over a human decision."},"role":"Manager","promptAppropriate":true},{"id":"D15","decision":"dont","facts":["A","C","T"],"label":"Employee feedback","title":"Summarizing a small-team survey","scenario":"An Academic Chair receives anonymous comments from a four-person team. Several comments describe distinctive incidents and job responsibilities.","prompt":"I'm an Academic Chair using Saskatchewan Polytechnic's approved Copilot environment. I have anonymous comments from a four-person team, and some comments describe distinctive incidents or job responsibilities. Help me produce an aggregated, paraphrased summary that reduces re-identification risk. Flag any comment that is too distinctive to safely reproduce verbatim.","response":"The Saskatchewan Polytechnic Code of Conduct requires confidentiality and responsible handling of information. Since the survey is anonymous and no names are attached, the comments can be shared verbatim without creating a privacy concern.","uneasy":"The prompt asked Copilot to reduce re-identification risk, but the response did the opposite: it treated removal of names as sufficient and endorsed verbatim sharing.","why":["Copilot assumes removal of names is enough to prevent identification in a very small group.","The leader needs to assess re-identification risk and whether paraphrasing, aggregation, or withholding distinctive comments is more appropriate.","A general Code of Conduct reference does not, by itself, establish that verbatim disclosure is safe; the applicable privacy/confidentiality requirements must actually support the approach."],"source":"Check: applicable privacy/confidentiality guidance, survey purpose/commitments, and institutional expectations for handling employee feedback.","better":"A reliable response would aggregate themes, paraphrase distinctive comments, and flag material that could identify a respondent.","sourceRefs":["Saskatchewan Polytechnic Code of Conduct — Policy #703"],"domain":"communication","nonPrimaryReasons":{"F":"The response's claim that anonymity eliminates the privacy concern is treated here as an assumption about re-identification risk. The underlying facts — four people, distinctive incidents, and removed names — are already supplied.","S":"The user is seeking advice about whether comments can be shared, and Copilot may assist with that analysis. The core problem is the unsafe assumption about anonymity, the need to assess re-identification risk, and the need to check the appropriate privacy requirements."},"role":"Academic Chair","promptAppropriate":true}];

const meta = {
  F:["Facts","Did Copilot state or rely on something as a fact that is not actually established?"],
  A:["Assumptions","Did Copilot infer, fill in, or treat something as true without enough evidence?"],
  C:["Confirm","Is there important information that needs to be checked or verified before relying on the response?"],
  T:["Trusted source","Did Copilot use the authoritative source that should govern this answer — and does the cited source actually support the conclusion?"],
  S:["Still mine","Is Copilot making or implying a judgment, exercising authority, or making a decision that belongs to a human?"]
};

function shuffle(array){
  const a=[...array];
  for(let i=a.length-1;i>0;i--){
    const j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));
    [a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]];
  }
  return a;
}

function selectFive(){
  const available=scenarioBank.filter(c=>!seenScenarioIds.has(c.id));

  if(available.length===0){
    return [];
  }

  // Draw up to five scenarios at random from those not yet encountered
  // in the current session. No scenario repeats until the pool is exhausted.
  return shuffle(available).slice(0,Math.min(5,available.length));
}

function markSeen(selected){
  selected.forEach(c=>seenScenarioIds.add(c.id));
}

let seenScenarioIds=new Set();
let cases=selectFive();
markSeen(cases);
let current=0;
let firstDecision=Array(cases.length).fill(null);
let activityStarted=false;

function beginActivity(){
  activityStarted=true;
  document.getElementById('beginBtn').textContent='Return to Activity';
  document.getElementById('introScreen').classList.add('hidden');
  document.getElementById('activityScreen').classList.remove('hidden');
  window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'});
}

function returnToDirections(){
  if(activityStarted){
    document.getElementById('beginBtn').textContent='Return to Activity';
  }
  document.getElementById('activityScreen').classList.add('hidden');
  document.getElementById('introScreen').classList.remove('hidden');
  window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'});
}

function buildKey(){
  const k=document.getElementById('key');
  k.innerHTML='';
  Object.entries(meta).forEach(([l,v])=>{
    k.innerHTML+=`<div class="keyitem"><div class="keyhead"><span class="letter">${l}</span>${v[0]}</div><p>${v[1]}</p></div>`;
  });
}

function render(){
  const c=cases[current];
  document.getElementById('round').textContent=`Case ${current+1} of ${cases.length}`;
  document.getElementById('fill').style.width=`${current/cases.length*100}%`;
  document.getElementById('label').textContent=`${c.role} • ${c.label}`;
  document.getElementById('title').textContent=c.title;
  document.getElementById('promptText').textContent=c.prompt;
  document.getElementById('response').textContent=c.response;

  const refsBox=document.getElementById('responseRefs');
  if(c.sourceRefs && c.sourceRefs.length){
    refsBox.innerHTML=`<div class="copilotrefs"><div class="copilotrefs-title">References cited by Copilot</div>${c.sourceRefs.map(r=>`<span class="refchip">${r}</span>`).join('')}</div>`;
  }else{
    refsBox.innerHTML=`<div class="noref">No source cited in this response.</div>`;
  }

  document.getElementById('feedback').className='feedback hidden';
  document.getElementById('feedback').innerHTML='';
  document.getElementById('next').classList.add('hidden');
  document.getElementById('sign').disabled=false;
  document.getElementById('dont').disabled=false;
}

function show(type,html){
  const f=document.getElementById('feedback');
  f.className=`feedback ${type}`;
  f.innerHTML=html;
}

function choose(choice){
  const c=cases[current];
  if(firstDecision[current]===null) firstDecision[current]=choice;

  document.getElementById('sign').disabled=true;
  document.getElementById('dont').disabled=true;

  const aligned=choice===c.decision;

  if(c.decision==='dont'){
    show(aligned?'good':'bad',`
      <h3>${aligned ? "Good call — don't sign it." : "This response deserves more caution."}</h3>
      <p><strong>The prompt was an appropriate use of Copilot. The problem is the response.</strong></p>\n      <p><strong>Why this response should make you uneasy:</strong> ${c.uneasy}</p>
      <div class="reflection-detail">
        <strong>What to notice:</strong>
        <ul>${c.why.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join('')}</ul>
      </div>
      <div class="source"><strong>${c.source}</strong></div>
      <div class="better"><strong>Better use of Copilot:</strong><br>${c.better}</div>
    `);
  }else{
    show(aligned?'good':'bad',`
      <h3>${aligned ? "Yes — this is signable." : "This response is appropriate to sign."}</h3>
      <p>This response stays within an appropriate support role for Copilot. It still requires the normal level of human review and verification appropriate to the task.</p>
      <div class="reflection-detail">
        <strong>Why it is appropriate:</strong>
        <ul>${c.why.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join('')}</ul>
      </div>
      <div class="source"><strong>${c.source}</strong></div>
      <div class="better"><strong>Good practice:</strong><br>${c.better}</div>
    `);
  }

  document.getElementById('next').textContent=current===cases.length-1?'Finish':'Next Case';
  document.getElementById('next').classList.remove('hidden');
}

function nextCase(){
  if(current<cases.length-1){
    current++;
    render();
  }else{
    showPerformanceSummary();
    document.getElementById('game').classList.add('hidden');
    document.getElementById('complete').classList.remove('hidden');
    updateRestartAvailability();
  }
}

function restartActivity(){
  const nextCases=selectFive();

  if(nextCases.length===0){
    updateRestartAvailability();
    return;
  }

  cases=nextCases;
  markSeen(cases);
  current=0;
  firstDecision=Array(cases.length).fill(null);

  document.getElementById('complete').classList.add('hidden');
  document.getElementById('game').classList.remove('hidden');
  document.getElementById('fill').style.width='0%';

  render();
  window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'});
}

function updateRestartAvailability(){
  const remaining=scenarioBank.filter(c=>!seenScenarioIds.has(c.id)).length;
  const btn=document.getElementById('restartBtn');
  const status=document.getElementById('restartStatus');

  if(remaining>0){
    btn.disabled=false;
    btn.textContent='Try 5 more';
    const nextCount=Math.min(5,remaining);
    status.textContent=remaining+' unseen scenario'+(remaining===1?'':'s')+' remain. Continue for another random set of '+nextCount+'.';
  }else{
    btn.disabled=true;
    btn.textContent='All Scenarios Completed';
    status.textContent='You have encountered all available scenarios in this session.';
  }
}

function showPerformanceSummary(){
  const aligned=cases.filter((c,i)=>firstDecision[i]===c.decision).length;
  const acceptedRisky=cases.filter((c,i)=>c.decision==='dont' && firstDecision[i]==='sign');
  const rejectedSound=cases.filter((c,i)=>c.decision==='sign' && firstDecision[i]==='dont');

  let overall;
  if(aligned===5){
    overall="Your judgment aligned with the activity across all five scenarios. You consistently distinguished between useful AI support and responses that reached beyond the evidence, source, or authority available.";
  }else if(aligned===4){
    overall="Your judgment aligned with the activity in four of the five scenarios. Overall, you showed a strong ability to distinguish useful Copilot support from responses that warranted more caution.";
  }else if(aligned===3){
    overall="Your responses showed a mixed but useful pattern. You recognized several important boundaries, while some polished or confident responses still earned either more or less trust than intended.";
  }else{
    overall=`Your judgment aligned with the activity in ${aligned} of the five scenarios. Some confident AI responses may be earning trust before their evidence, source, or authority has been fully tested — or appropriately bounded uses of Copilot may feel riskier than they need to.`;
  }

  let pattern;
  if(acceptedRisky.length && rejectedSound.length){
    pattern="You showed both kinds of hesitation: sometimes accepting a response that needed more scrutiny, and sometimes rejecting a response that was appropriately bounded. Focus less on whether AI was used and more on what role it is being asked to play.";
  }else if(acceptedRisky.length){
    pattern="When your judgment differed from the activity, the pattern leaned toward accepting responses that needed more scrutiny. Watch especially for confident recommendations, authoritative-looking citations, and conclusions that move beyond the facts provided.";
  }else if(rejectedSound.length){
    pattern="When your judgment differed from the activity, the pattern leaned toward being more cautious than necessary. Copilot can be useful when it is organizing, comparing, drafting, or surfacing questions while keeping consequential judgment with the human leader.";
  }else{
    pattern="You consistently applied the right question: not simply 'Did AI produce this?' but 'Would I be comfortable taking responsibility for relying on it?'";
  }

  document.getElementById('performanceSummary').innerHTML=`
    <div class="summarycard">
      <h3>Your performance summary</h3>
      <div class="summarysection">
        <strong>Overall judgment</strong>
        ${overall}
      </div>
      <div class="summarysection">
        <strong>Your decision pattern</strong>
        ${pattern}
      </div>
      <div class="summarynote">The prompts in this activity were all appropriate uses of Copilot. The skill is deciding how much confidence the response deserves: verify facts, check sources, notice assumptions, and keep consequential judgment with the accountable human.</div>
    </div>`;
}

buildKey();
render();
